<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karen people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karen are a group of related peoples speaking Karenic languages, living mainly in the hills of south-eastern Myanmar along the Thai border, with communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Many became Christians through 19th-century Baptist missions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-national-union">Karen National Union</a> began an armed struggle for autonomy in 1949, one of the world's longest-running insurgencies, and hundreds of thousands of Karen have fled to camps in Thailand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karen-people.jpg" alt="Karen people" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: R. Lenz. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Karen_woman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Karen, also called the Kayin, consist of about 20 subgroups, the largest of which are the S'gaw and the Pwo. A pan-Karen identity is a relatively modern creation, established in the 19th century with the conversion of some Karen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and shaped by British colonial policies. In pre-colonial times the Burmese and Mon kingdoms recognised two categories: the lowland Pwo, called Talaing Kayin, regarded as original settlers, and the highland S'gaw, called Bamar Kayin, who were subordinated or assimilated by the Bamar.</p><p>Karen legends speak of a river of running sand that their ancestors crossed. Many Karen take it to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a>, but most scholars read it as rivers carrying sand, perhaps the sediment-laden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. The linguists Luce and Lehman estimate that Tibeto-Burman peoples such as the Karen migrated into present-day Myanmar between 300 and 800 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Baptist missionaries brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to Myanmar from 1830 and converted many Karen. The first Karen convert, Tha Byu, was baptised in 1828 by George Boardman. After Myanmar was annexed as a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1886, the colonial authorities favoured Christian Karen, who gained military recruitment and seats in the legislature. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> many Karen villages were destroyed and massacres committed by both the Japanese and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a>, and Karen soldiers supported the British in Operation Character in 1945.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglong-conference">Panglong Conference</a> of 1947 the Karen attended only as observers. In February 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-national-union">Karen National Union</a> was formed at a Karen Congress of 700 delegates. After independence in January 1948 it tried at first to coexist peacefully with the Burman majority. In January 1949 the Karen general Smith Dun was removed as army chief of staff, and the Karen National Defence Organisation rose in insurgency, coming close to capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> and holding out for 111 days at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/insein">Insein</a>. The union first aimed at an independent Karen homeland called Kawthoolei and since 1976 has called for a federal system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Karen languages are Tibeto-Burman and comprise three mutually unintelligible branches: Sgaw, Eastern Pwo and Western Pwo. They are unusual in the family in having subject-verb-object word order, probably through the influence of Mon and Tai languages, and are written in a modified form of the Mon-Burmese script.</p><p>Most Karen are Theravada Buddhists who also practise animism, and about 15 to 30 per cent are Christian. Karen animism holds that every person is embodied by thirty-seven spirits, called k'lar, and that death comes when all of them leave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karen peoples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom's valleys were separated by forested hills inhabited by other peoples, including Lawa and <b>Karen</b>, who traded forest products with the lowlanders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bamar, or Burmans, of the central Irrawaddy valley form about two-thirds of the population; the Shan, <b>Karen</b>, Rakhine, Mon, Chin, Kachin and many smaller groups live mostly in the hills and borderlands that ring the plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1740 the Mon of Lower Burma, together with <b>Karen</b> and Burmese allies, rebelled against the weakened Restored Toungoo Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/restored-hanthawaddy-kingdom">Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
